Good plan — I can help you organize what to ask and what to check at your annual physical so you and Dr. Chen can make a clear decision about bone health and referrals. Below I’ve summarized the key issues given what you shared, listed specific questions to bring to the visit, suggested tests and data to gather beforehand, and practical next steps (including when an orthopedics referral is appropriate).

Key points from what you told me
- Age 55 with atrial fibrillation (you mentioned an INR of 1.2) — please confirm whether you are on warfarin or a direct oral anticoagulant (DOAC). INR 1.2 is low for someone on warfarin; if you are not on warfarin that value is just a lab baseline.
- Taking metformin 500 mg twice daily — suggests type 2 diabetes (important because diabetes increases fracture risk even if BMD isn’t low).
- Taking calcium and vitamin D for seasonal allergies? (I assume you mean you also take calcium + vitamin D supplements — tell me doses.)
- History of retinopathy — consistent with diabetes and relevant to overall fracture risk/falls.
- PCP: Dr. Sarah Chen, Denver Heart Institute — coordination with cardiology may be needed for anticoagulation questions.

What to confirm before the visit (quick items to gather)
- Are you on any anticoagulant? If so, which one and what is your target INR (if warfarin)? When was your last dose/last INR check?
- Exact doses of calcium and vitamin D you’re taking.
- Any prior DEXA (bone density) scans and reports (date and results/T-scores).
- Any prior fragility fractures (low trauma fractures, e.g., wrist, spine, hip).
- Height and weight (used for FRAX).
- Current HbA1c or recent diabetes labs, and renal function (most recent creatinine/eGFR).
- Any other meds that affect bone (chronic steroids, SSRIs, anticonvulsants, proton-pump inhibitors, thyroid hormone overtreatment, etc.)
- Falls history or balance problems.

Tests and assessments to consider ordering at/around the visit
- DEXA scan (central femoral neck + lumbar spine) if you haven’t had one recently — this is the central test for osteopenia/osteoporosis.
- FRAX 10-year fracture risk calculation (requires age, sex, weight, height, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, smoking, glucocorticoids, RA, secondary osteoporosis, alcohol).
- Labs:
  - 25-hydroxyvitamin D level
  - Serum calcium
  - Creatinine/eGFR (kidney function matters for medication choices)
  - Basic metabolic panel/CMP
  - CBC if clinically indicated
  - HbA1c (given metformin/retinopathy)
  - TSH and/or others if suggested by history
  - PTH if calcium is abnormal
- Dental evaluation if you may start antiresorptive therapy (especially IV bisphosphonate or denosumab) — to reduce osteonecrosis of the jaw risk.

How clinicians usually decide whether to treat osteopenia
- Treatment depends on DEXA T-score and fracture risk:
  - Osteoporosis: T-score ≤ −2.5 — usually treat.
  - Osteopenia (T-score between −1.0 and −2.5): consider treatment if FRAX 10-year major osteoporotic fracture risk is high (commonly used thresholds: ≥20% major or ≥3% hip risk), or if other high-risk features exist (prior fragility fracture, high falls risk, secondary causes).
- Diabetes and retinopathy raise fracture risk even if BMD is only osteopenic — this is important when interpreting FRAX/DXA.
- Renal function influences which medications are safe (some bisphosphonates have eGFR cutoffs).

Medication options and relevant considerations
- Oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate): typical first-line for many patients with low BMD. Must follow dosing instructions (fasting, upright, no food for 30–60 min). Caution in significant renal impairment.
- IV bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid): option if oral not tolerated; check renal function before infusion.
- Denosumab (Prolia): 6-month subcutaneous injection; effective for high risk but requires on-time dosing; injection site bleeding risk if you’re anticoagulated — coordinate with your anticoagulation provider.
- Anabolic agents (teriparatide, romosozumab): for very high-risk patients or those with multiple fractures — usually started by specialty (endocrinology).
- Dental check before starting antiresorptives (IV bisphosphonates or denosumab).
- If you’re on warfarin: long‑term warfarin has been associated in some studies with altered bone health — review with your providers. If on DOACs, the anticoagulation issue is different (no INR monitoring).

Anticoagulation coordination
- Confirm whether you are on warfarin vs. DOAC. If warfarin and INR target is 2–3, an INR of 1.2 is subtherapeutic — discuss with cardiology/anticoagulation clinic.
- For injections (e.g., denosumab) or procedures, discuss whether anticoagulation needs adjustment to reduce hematoma risk.
- DEXA scans and most routine labs are safe regardless of anticoagulation.

When to consider referral
- Referral to orthopedics is usually indicated for acute fractures, structural joint problems, or surgical considerations — not typically for primary osteoporosis management.
- Consider referral to a metabolic bone specialist, endocrinologist, or osteoporosis clinic if:
  - You have fragility fractures.
  - Very low T-scores (≤ −2.5) or multiple risk factors.
  - Complex secondary causes (e.g., severe renal disease, history of atypical fractures).
  - You need anabolic therapy or have failed/are intolerant of first-line therapies.
- If you’re concerned about fall mechanics, balance, or gait, a referral to physical therapy or geriatrics for fall-prevention and strength/balance training is often more helpful than orthopedics.
- If you want an orthopedist opinion specifically because of joint pain, prior fractures, or planned surgery — then yes, refer to Northwest Orthopedics.

Practical prevention steps you can discuss and start now
- Weight-bearing and resistance exercise program.
- Home fall-risk assessment (remove trip hazards, improve lighting, review vision and medications that cause dizziness).
- Ensure total calcium intake is about 1,000–1,200 mg/day (diet + supplement); avoid taking high-dose calcium at the same time as some meds — ask Dr. Chen about timing.
- Vitamin D: commonly 800–1,000 IU/day or higher if deficient — get level checked and adjust dose accordingly.
- Review blood sugar control and retinopathy follow-up (better glycemic control lowers long-term fracture risk indirectly).
- Tobacco cessation and limit alcohol (≥3 drinks/day increases fracture risk).

Suggested questions to bring to Dr. Chen
Assessment and testing
- “Given my age (55), history of atrial fibrillation, diabetes (metformin), and retinopathy, do I need a DEXA scan now, and can you calculate my FRAX score?”
- “Please order 25-OH vitamin D, serum calcium, creatinine/eGFR, and HbA1c if not done recently.”
- “Do I meet criteria for pharmacologic treatment for osteopenia/osteoporosis?”

Medication and safety
- “If treatment is recommended, what medication do you suggest and why? What are the pros and cons (oral bisphosphonate vs. denosumab vs. others) given my medical history?”
- “I have an INR of 1.2—am I on warfarin or a DOAC? If I start denosumab or need injections, how should we coordinate with my anticoagulation?”

Referrals and specialists
- “Would you refer me to a metabolic bone specialist/endocrinology or to Northwest Orthopedics? If so, for which concerns would orthopedics be most appropriate?”
- “Should I see dentistry before starting bone medications?”

Pre-visit logistics
- “Can you review my current calcium and vitamin D doses and confirm if they’re sufficient or if I need a blood test before changing doses?”
- “Are there specific medications I should stop before testing or treatment (or that might affect bone)?”
- “If you recommend starting treatment, what monitoring will we do (labs, repeat DEXA frequency)?”

Offer I can help with
- If you’d like, I can draft a concise message or phone note to Dr. Chen summarizing these questions and asking for a DEXA and the listed labs.
- I can also help you make a short checklist to bring to the appointment listing your meds/doses, prior test dates, and the top 3 questions you want answered.

Next question for you
- Are you currently on an anticoagulant (warfarin or a DOAC)? What is the exact dose of calcium and vitamin D you take, and do you have a prior DEXA or recent HbA1c/creatinine I should know about? With that I can tailor the questions and, if you want, draft the message to Dr. Chen.

