Good — I can help you prepare focused questions and a plan to discuss osteopenia and whether a referral is needed. I’m not a doctor, but below is up-to-date, practical information you can take to Dr. Sarah Chen plus suggested questions and a short checklist for your appointment.

Key points about your situation
- Age 55 — fracture risk rises with age.
- Type 2 diabetes (on metformin) and history of diabetic retinopathy — both increase fracture/fall risk (retinopathy increases fall risk; diabetes is associated with higher fracture risk even if BMD is not low).
- You take calcium and vitamin D supplements — good baseline step. Confirm doses.
- INR 1.2 — if you are on warfarin, that INR is subtherapeutic for atrial fibrillation anticoagulation (typical target 2.0–3.0). If you’re on a DOAC instead, INR isn’t relevant. Ask your PCP about this — it affects peri-procedure planning and overall risk management.
- Orthopedics vs primary/specialty bone care: Orthopedics treats fractures and mechanical problems. Osteopenia without fracture is usually managed by PCP, endocrinology, or a bone health/osteoporosis clinic unless you have a fracture, severe bone pain, or an orthopedic structural issue.

Tests and assessments to consider before or at the visit
- DEXA (bone mineral density) if you haven’t had one recently — get T-scores and hip/spine measurements. If last DEXA >2 years ago or never, ask to schedule.
- Calculate 10-year fracture risk (FRAX). Note: FRAX may under-estimate risk in people with diabetes; tell the clinician you have diabetes.
- Labs to evaluate secondary causes and safety of treatment:
  - 25-hydroxyvitamin D
  - Serum calcium, creatinine (kidney function), and electrolytes
  - TSH (thyroid)
  - CBC (if indicated)
  - Parathyroid hormone, if abnormal calcium
  - Consider tests for celiac disease, SPEP, or testosterone (men) if clinician suspects secondary causes
- Fall risk assessment: vision, gait/balance, home safety, medications that increase fall risk.
- Review dental history if bisphosphonate therapy is being considered (rare risk of osteonecrosis of the jaw).

When to consider pharmacologic treatment (general guidance)
- Treatment is generally recommended if T-score ≤ −2.5 (osteoporosis) or if you have a fragility fracture.
- For osteopenia (T-score between −1.0 and −2.5), treatment is considered if FRAX 10-year major osteoporotic fracture risk is above guideline thresholds (varies, e.g., major osteoporotic risk ≥20% or hip ≥3% in some guidelines), or if there are additional risk factors (long-term glucocorticoids, prior fragility fracture, very high risk diabetes/retinopathy, frailty).
- Because diabetes and retinopathy increase risk, discuss whether your clinician considers you higher risk than FRAX indicates.

Common treatment options and considerations
- Lifestyle: weight-bearing & resistance exercise, smoking cessation, alcohol moderation, fall-prevention strategies, vision optimization.
