- Exact doses of calcium and vitamin D supplements.
- Menopause status (age at menopause, if applicable) and any history of estrogen or hormone therapy.
- Recent labs: serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D, CBC, CMP (including creatinine for renal function), calcium, TSH. Consider B12 (metformin can cause B12 deficiency), and if abnormalities, PTH or other tests as indicated.
- If you’re on anticoagulation: which drug (warfarin vs DOAC) and target INR or recent anticoagulation monitoring record.

Tests and measures to consider ordering
- DEXA scan (if none or outdated)
- FRAX with BMD
- Serum 25-OH vitamin D, calcium, creatinine, albumin (to correct calcium), TSH, CBC
- Vitamin B12 if on long-term metformin
- Consider fall-risk assessment (gait, balance), vision check if not done recently
- If atypical labs or secondary osteoporosis suspected: PTH, SPEP (if suspicious for myeloma), morning cortisol if Cushing suspected

Key questions to ask Dr. Chen (phrased for the visit)
- “Do I need a DEXA scan now? If so, can you order it and calculate my FRAX score?”
- “Given my age, atrial fibrillation, history of retinopathy, and (possible) diabetes, what is my 10-year fracture risk and do you recommend starting drug treatment for osteopenia?”
- “What are my current calcium and vitamin D levels? Are my supplement doses appropriate?”

- “Which medications might be contributing to bone loss that I’m taking or should avoid?”
- “Do I need any additional labs (B12, PTH, thyroid, renal) before deciding on treatment?”
- “Is my INR of 1.2 a concern? Which anticoagulant am I using, and does that affect bone testing or treatment options?”
- “Would you recommend a referral to an osteoporosis specialist (endocrinology/rheumatology/bone health clinic) vs orthopedics? If referral is needed, which type of specialist should I see?”
- “What fall-prevention measures or physical therapy would you recommend given my retinopathy?”
- “If medication is recommended, can you explain the benefits and side effects of bisphosphonates, denosumab, or other options?”

When to treat osteopenia (general thresholds and factors)
- Osteopenia (T-score −1.0 to −2.5) is common; treatment decisions are individualized.
- Typical treatment indications include:
  - T-score ≤ −2.5 (osteoporosis) — generally treat
  - History of a fragility fracture — treat
  - FRAX 10-year risk above guideline thresholds (commonly ≥3% hip fracture risk or ≥20% major osteoporotic fracture risk) — treat
  - High-risk secondary causes (long-term steroids, uncontrolled endocrine disease, recurrent falls)
- Your age, diabetic status and retinopathy increase fracture/fall risk; these factors can push toward treatment even with osteopenia.

Medication options — pros/cons and monitoring (talk these over with Dr. Chen)
- Oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate)
  - Pros: proven fracture risk reduction, inexpensive
  - Cons: esophageal irritation (need to take upright with water and remain upright 30–60 minutes), contraindicated with poor renal function; rare risks: osteonecrosis of the jaw (mostly with dental procedures/IV use) and atypical femur fracture (rare)
  - Monitoring: periodic BMD, renal function
- IV bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid)
  - Pros: convenient yearly dosing
  - Cons: flu-like infusion reactions, renal dosing considerations, dental precautions
- Denosumab (Prolia)
  - Pros: effective, given subcutaneously every 6 months
  - Cons: rebound bone loss if stopped without alternative therapy; dental precautions; infection risk considerations
- Anabolic agents (teriparatide, abaloparatide) — for severe cases or refractory osteoporosis
- SERMs (raloxifene) — considered in some postmenopausal women (reduces vertebral fractures)
- General: ensure adequate vitamin D and calcium intake (but avoid excessive calcium supplements). Check kidney function before some therapies.

Non-drug management to discuss
- Weight-bearing and resistance exercise, balance training or PT
- Fall prevention: home safety, vision optimization, review of sedating meds
- Smoking cessation, limit alcohol
- Adequate protein and nutrition
- Dental health: inform dentist if starting antiresorptives; treat major dental issues before IV bisphosphonates

When to refer to orthopedics vs osteoporosis specialist
- Orthopedics referral appropriate for: an acute fracture, deformity/structural joint issues, orthopedic surgical management needs (hip/knee replacement, fracture fixation), or pain from a musculoskeletal injury.
- For osteopenia/osteoporosis management without fracture, referral is typically to:
  - Endocrinology or a metabolic bone clinic or rheumatology for complex/secondary osteoporosis, intolerance to first-line meds, or need for anabolic therapy.
  - Physical therapy for balance/strength and fall prevention.
- Based on what you described (age 55, osteopenia risk factors, no acute fracture reported), an osteoporosis specialist or bone clinic would be more appropriate than orthopedics unless you have a fracture or mechanical joint problem.

Specific things to mention because they matter in decision-making
- Confirm whether you have diabetes and most recent A1C (diabetes increases fracture risk).
- Confirm anticoagulant type (warfarin vs DOAC). If you are on warfarin, an INR of 1.2 is low — mention this to your PCP urgently because it may be subtherapeutic for stroke prevention and may affect procedural planning.
- Provide exact supplement doses (mg of calcium, IU of vitamin D).
- Recent dental history (if dental work planned, tell provider before starting IV antiresorptives).

Suggested visit plan (short checklist to take with you)
- Bring current med list and supplement doses
- Ask provider to order DEXA and FRAX if none recently done
- Request labs: 25-OH vitamin D, CMP, calcium, TSH, CBC, B12 (if on metformin)
- Discuss whether to start medication now or wait for test results
- Ask about fall prevention/PT and schedule ophthalmology follow-up if vision has changed
- Clarify anticoagulant status and INR management

Would you like any of the following?
