Recommended tests to request (bring these up at the visit)
- DEXA scan (lumbar spine + femoral neck) and FRAX calculation (with femoral neck BMD if available)
- Labs: 25-hydroxyvitamin D, serum calcium, creatinine and estimated GFR, phosphorus, PTH (if indicated), TSH, CBC, alkaline phosphatase. Consider B12 (metformin can cause deficiency), and celiac screening if clinically suspected.
- If abnormal labs or unexplained bone loss: consider protein electrophoresis, testosterone (men), estradiol (women if premenopausal concerns), or referral to endocrinology.

How your specific issues affect bone management
- Atrial fibrillation / anticoagulation: INR 1.2 is below the usual therapeutic range for warfarin (commonly 2.0–3.0 for AF). Discuss anticoagulation management with your PCP. Anticoagulation does not usually prevent starting oral bisphosphonates, but dental procedures (risk of osteonecrosis of the jaw with anti-resorptives) need coordination if you are anticoagulated. IV bisphosphonates require checking renal function.
- Metformin: long-term metformin can cause vitamin B12 deficiency, which can increase neuropathy/fall risk — check B12.
- Retinopathy: visual impairment increases fall risk and fracture risk; ensure up-to-date eye care and fall-prevention measures.
- Calcium + vitamin D: confirm total daily calcium intake (diet + supplements). Typical targets: total calcium ~1,000–1,200 mg/day and vitamin D ~800–1000 IU/day OR adjust after checking level.

Treatment thresholds and options (brief)
- Osteopenia vs osteoporosis: DEXA T-score -1.0 to -2.5 = osteopenia; ≤ -2.5 = osteoporosis.
- Many guidelines recommend pharmacologic treatment for osteopenia only if FRAX 10-year hip fracture risk ≥3% or major osteoporotic fracture risk ≥20%, or if there are prior fragility fractures or other high-risk features.
- First-line meds: oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate) if no contraindications and GFR adequate. IV zoledronic acid or subcutaneous denosumab are options for intolerance/absorption/adherence problems or when needing stronger therapy.
- Before anti-resorptives: check kidney function, dental health (evaluate for invasive dental work), and discuss risks (esophagitis with oral bisphosphonates, rare atypical femoral fracture, osteonecrosis of the jaw).

When to refer to orthopedics vs endocrinology/bone clinic
- Orthopedics: appropriate if you have a recent fragility fracture, acute severe bone/joint pain needing surgical evaluation, or structural problems.
- Endocrinology / bone-osteoporosis clinic: appropriate for complex secondary osteoporosis, very low BMD, multiple fractures, intolerance to standard therapies, or when advanced options (IV therapies, bone anabolic agents) are being considered.
- In most cases of osteopenia without fracture, PCP can manage or refer to endocrinology if treatment decisions are complex.

Practical items to bring to your visit
- List of all medications (including doses and supplements) and date of last INR or anticoagulation clinic records.
- Any prior DEXA reports or imaging.
- History of falls, fractures, family history of hip fracturing.
- Recent lab results (A1c, creatinine, vitamin D if available).

Lifestyle and prevention tips to discuss
- Weight-bearing and resistance exercises, balance training (e.g., Tai Chi), home fall-proofing, smoking cessation, limit alcohol.
- Ensure adequate protein intake and maintain healthy body weight.
- Vision optimization and foot/neuropathy checks (if diabetic).

