I can help — I’m not a doctor, but here’s a practical, evidence-based checklist and suggested questions to take to your PCP (Dr. Sarah Chen) so you can decide about osteopenia management and whether an orthopedic referral makes sense.

Quick context notes from what you said
- Age 55 — osteopenia risk rises after midlife.
- Diabetes (on metformin) and retinopathy — both increase fall and fracture risk indirectly.
- Atrial fibrillation and you mentioned an INR of 1.2 — that suggests you may be on warfarin (INR 1.2 is low for AF). Please confirm which anticoagulant you’re taking (warfarin vs a DOAC).
- Current supplements: calcium and vitamin D (please tell me doses).

What to clarify before decisions
- Which anticoagulant (warfarin or DOAC) and current anticoagulation plan? (If warfarin, bring up the low INR.)
- Any prior DEXA / bone density results (dates & T-scores)?
- Any history of fragility/low-trauma fractures (hip, vertebra, wrist)?
- Current calcium and vitamin D doses, smoking/alcohol, fall history, mobility, and kidney function (recent creatinine/eGFR).
- Sex / menopausal status (if female: years since menopause), since management differs.

Tests your PCP may order or review
- DEXA (bone mineral density) if not done in last 1–2 years (or sooner if rapid loss or fracture).
- FRAX 10-year fracture risk (uses DEXA + clinical risk factors).
- Labs: CMP (creatinine, calcium), 25-hydroxyvitamin D, TSH, CBC; HbA1c if diabetes, and possibly serum protein electrophoresis or other tests if secondary causes suspected.
- Fall-risk evaluation (vision, orthostatics, gait/balance).

When medical treatment is recommended (general thresholds)
- Start medication if DEXA shows osteoporosis (T-score ≤ -2.5) or if osteopenia with high FRAX risk (commonly used thresholds: 10-year major osteoporotic fracture ≥20% or hip fracture ≥3% — confirm how your PCP interprets FRAX).
- If osteopenia but low FRAX risk, focus on lifestyle, supplements, monitoring DEXA at regular intervals (often 1–2 years), and fall prevention.

Medication options and issues to discuss
- Oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate): first-line for many. Contraindicated or used cautiously if eGFR is low; GI side effects possible.
- IV bisphosphonate (zoledronic acid): one infusion yearly; consider dental evaluation beforehand because of a small risk of osteonecrosis of the jaw.
- Denosumab (Prolia): effective, but stopping abruptly can cause rapid bone loss — requires a plan for follow-up therapy.
- Anabolic agents (teriparatide, abaloparatide) for severe cases or multiple fractures.
- Raloxifene if vertebral-fracture risk and for some postmenopausal patients.
- Discuss side effects: atypical femoral fracture (rare), osteonecrosis of the jaw (rare), renal considerations, and GI issues.

How your AF/anticoagulation affects bone care
- If you’re on warfarin, INR 1.2 is subtherapeutic for AF — mention this to your cardiologist/PCP (risk of stroke).
- Anticoagulation does not generally prevent use of osteoporosis medications, but procedural planning (dental extraction, any invasive procedure) should be coordinated with your anticoagulation plan.
- Some evidence links long-term warfarin to higher fracture risk; it’s not an absolute contraindication but worth discussing.

When to refer to orthopedics (Northwest Orthopedics) vs a bone specialist
- Refer to orthopedics if you have a recent fracture, deformity, persistent severe bone/joint pain, or need surgical evaluation.
- For medical management of osteopenia/osteoporosis, consider referral to:
  - Endocrinology or a dedicated osteoporosis clinic if you have complex/secondary causes, very low bone density, multiple fractures, intolerance to first-line meds, or kidney impairment.
  - Geriatrics or physical therapy for falls, balance training, and strength programs.
- Given your age and risk factors, an orthopedic referral isn’t routinely required unless there’s a fracture or orthopedic problem. A bone specialist referral would be more appropriate for complicated medication decisions.

Practical lifestyle and fall-prevention steps to start now
- Ensure total calcium intake 1000–1200 mg/day (diet + supplement). Don’t exceed recommended amounts without checking.
- Vitamin D: many clinicians aim for 800–2000 IU/day depending on level; adjust based on 25-OH vitamin D lab.
- Weight-bearing and resistance exercises plus balance training (PT or Tai Chi) 2–3× weekly.
- Review medications that cause dizziness; treat vision issues (retinopathy raises fall risk).
- Home safety: remove trip hazards, improve lighting, consider grab bars if needed.
- Smoking cessation and limit alcohol (<2 drinks/day, ideally less).

