Thanks — this is a good start. Below I’ve pulled together a focused checklist you can use to prepare for your annual physical and specific questions to ask about osteopenia and whether you need an orthopedics referral. I also flag a few things to confirm before the visit.

Quick recap of what you told me
- Age: 55  
- Medical issues: atrial fibrillation, seasonal allergies, history of retinopathy  
- Meds you mentioned: metformin 500 mg twice daily; you also take calcium and vitamin D supplements  
- Last INR: 1.2  
- PCP: Dr. Sarah Chen, Denver Heart Institute

Before the visit — quick clarifying questions to answer (if you don’t know these, bring them up at the visit)
- What is your sex and menopausal status (post- or pre-menopausal)? (Important for fracture risk and DEXA interpretation.)
- Do you have a prior DEXA scan? If yes, date and T-scores (spine and hip).  
- Any history of fragility fractures (low-trauma wrist, hip, vertebral compression)?  
- Any falls in the last year or balance problems? Any limited mobility?  
- Full current medication list (especially anticoagulants — are you on warfarin or a DOAC, steroid use, SSRIs, PPIs, aromatase inhibitors)? INR 1.2 suggests you may be on warfarin — confirm.  
- Smoking status, alcohol use (drinks per week), family history of hip fracture, BMI/weight.  
- Recent labs: most recent HbA1c, creatinine/eGFR, calcium, 25‑OH vitamin D if available.

Tests and assessments to consider asking your PCP for
- DEXA (central hip and lumbar spine) if you haven’t had one within the last 1–2 years or if you don’t have a baseline.  
- FRAX 10‑year fracture risk calculation (with/without BMD).  
- Labs: serum 25‑OH vitamin D, serum calcium, CMP (including creatinine), TSH, and HbA1c (given diabetes). Consider 24‑hr urine calcium if indicated.  
- Falls risk screen and, if positive, referral to physical therapy or balance training. Visual acuity check given history of retinopathy.  
- Review of all meds for contributors to bone loss (oral steroids, anticonvulsants, some SSRIs, PPIs, warfarin possible effect).

Key management questions to ask Dr. Chen about osteopenia
- Do my DEXA results (or my FRAX score) indicate I should start medication to prevent fractures?  
- What is my current T‑score and 10‑year fracture risk (FRAX)? What thresholds are you using to recommend treatment? (Common criteria: T‑score ≤ −2.5, prior fragility fracture, or FRAX risk above guideline thresholds — but thresholds vary by guideline/country.)  
- Do I need vitamin D repletion or a different dose than I’m taking? What target 25‑OH vitamin D level should we aim for?  
- Is my calcium intake adequate (diet + supplements)? Aim typically ~1000–1200 mg/day depending on age/sex — ask for individualized guidance.  
- Are there medications I should stop or change that might worsen bone health?  
- If medication is recommended, what are the options (oral bisphosphonate like alendronate vs. denosumab, etc.), benefits, risks, monitoring, and dental precautions? (Discuss dental exam before starting bisphosphonates if indicated.)  
- How often should we repeat DEXA if I have osteopenia? (Often every 1–2 years initially, then longer intervals if stable.)  
- Should we evaluate for secondary causes of low bone density (e.g., thyroid disease, vitamin D deficiency, chronic kidney disease)?

When an orthopedic referral is appropriate (versus primary care / endocrinology / bone clinic)
- Refer to Orthopedics (Northwest Orthopedics) if: you’ve had a recent fracture that may need surgical care (hip, wrist, vertebral compression causing significant pain), new or worsening focal bone/joint pain suggesting a structural problem, or if you need surgical evaluation.  
- For medical management of low bone density (osteopenia/osteoporosis without fracture), primary care can usually manage this; if things are complex consider referral to: endocrinology or a metabolic bone specialist, or an osteoporosis/bone health clinic.  
- If you have recurrent falls, significant mobility issues, or balance impairment, consider PT and possibly ortho if joint problems (hip/knee) are contributing.

Specific items relevant to your AFib / INR / retinopathy
- Clarify whether you’re on warfarin vs a DOAC. INR 1.2 is low if you are on warfarin and may need cardiology/anticoagulation follow-up. Anticoagulant type matters for fall/bleeding risk counseling and some peri‑procedure decisions.  
- Retinopathy increases fall risk; ask about vision‑related fall precautions and consider referral for low‑vision support if vision is impaired.  
- Diabetes control (HbA1c) matters for bone quality — discuss optimizing glycemic control.

Practical visit checklist you can bring
- List of current meds (including dose/frequency), supplements (calcium & vitamin D dose)  
- Dates/results of any prior DEXA, recent labs (HbA1c, CMP, vitamin D if available)  
- Notes on any falls, fractures, or mobility issues since your last visit  
- Specific questions from the “Key management” list above

Short scripts you can use at the appointment
- “I’m 55 with AFib and diabetic retinopathy. I’m concerned about osteopenia — do I need a DEXA or treatment now?”  
- “My last INR was 1.2; can we confirm my anticoagulant plan and whether that changes bone or fall-related recommendations?”  
- “If you recommend medication for bone health, what are the pros/cons and monitoring steps, and do I need a dental check before starting?”

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short email or message to Dr. Chen requesting a DEXA, vitamin D level, and review of bone health? OR
- Draft a one‑page summary you can print and bring to the visit with the checklist and questions?

I can also calculate an estimated FRAX if you give me: sex, weight, height, prior fracture history, parental hip fracture, smoking status, glucocorticoid use, rheumatoid arthritis, secondary osteoporosis, alcohol use (3+ drinks/day), and whether you have a femoral neck T‑score.

